{
  "name": "cross_domain_rejection_v1",
  "description": "Fixtures that every implementation must REJECT. Each case is a well-formed-looking object presented to the named task-approval validator. canonical_json is the canonical encoding of that object and is what the tests feed to the validator, so each rejection is attributable to the domain or role rule rather than to the canonical round trip. A case carrying raw_json instead is a byte-level fixture whose bytes cannot be expressed as a parsed object. Domain substitution and key-role substitution are the two ways a caller could try to make owner-control authority satisfy a task effect, or the reverse. ARM-46 replaced the former structural placeholder with a real armel.owner-control.manifest object. Not one rejection rule changed: they bind to the domain and role markers rather than to the object body, which is exactly what the placeholder seam was for. Cases formerly named ..._placeholder_as_... now present the real owner-control object to each task-approval validator.",
